[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]What is LibAsync?[/COLOR][/SIZE]
LibAsync spreads long-running Lua work over many frames, so the game stays smooth instead of freezing. It measures the current frame rate and gives your code only as much time per frame as the game can spare. This increases the total duration of your work, but avoids stutter, crashes from huge allocations and kicks from too many server requests at once.

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]For players[/COLOR][/SIZE]
Add-ons using LibAsync never push the frame rate below a lower limit. You can change it (v3.0.2+):
[CODE]/async stall <fps>     (15 - 200, default 15)
/async stall default[/CODE]
The time budget per frame is limited to 1/fps seconds, the rest of the work waits for the next frame. Normally the frame rate settles at 60, 30, 20 or 15 fps, depending on your system. Raise the value if you prefer smoother frames over faster completion. The budget is smaller while the HUD is visible than in menus. On console the add-on CPU budget is respected as well.
If your machine is overloaded by other things, the frame rate still could be even lower. Just saying.

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]For developers[/COLOR][/SIZE]
Add [FONT="Courier New"]## DependsOn: LibAsync[/FONT] to your manifest and use the global variable (LibStub is not supported since 2.3.4):
[CODE]local async = LibAsync
local task = async:Create(name)  -- name is optional[/CODE]
Read also [URL="https://wiki.esoui.com/Running_LUA-Code_asynchroneously"]this article[/URL] of the Wiki.

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]Core concepts[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]Every step is a [FONT="Courier New"]FuncOfTask[/FONT]: [CODE]local function(task)
end[/CODE]
[*]If a step returns [FONT="Courier New"]true[/FONT], it is called again in the next time slice, until it returns something else. This is how you build your own loops.
[*]Without [FONT="Courier New"]Create[/FONT], [FONT="Courier New"]async:Call/For/While/WaitUntil/Sort[/FONT] run on a shared default task: not cancellable, and [FONT="Courier New"]Cancel[/FONT], [FONT="Courier New"]Finally[/FONT] and [FONT="Courier New"]OnError[/FONT] raise an error there. Called from inside a running step they become nested calls of the current task.
[*]Task names must be unique. Two tasks with the same name replace each other. Omit the name to get a unique one.
[*]Errors: with [FONT="Courier New"]OnError[/FONT] the remaining steps are dropped and the handler is called. Without it the task is suspended and the error is rethrown. [FONT="Courier New"]Finally[/FONT] always runs: after success, after an error and after [FONT="Courier New"]Cancel[/FONT].
[/LIST]

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]API: Task control[/COLOR][/SIZE]
-- Get the current task, if you are within a FuncOfTask, otherwise nil.
[CODE]function async:GetCurrent()[/CODE]
-- Create an interruptible task.
[CODE]function async:Create(name)[/CODE]
-- Resume the task.
[CODE]function task:Resume()[/CODE]
-- Suspend the task. It can be resumed anytime later.
[CODE]function task:Suspend()[/CODE]
-- Interrupt and fully stop the task (also stops a pending Delay). Can be called from outside. Finally still runs.
[CODE]function task:Cancel()[/CODE]

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]API: Sequencing[/COLOR][/SIZE]
-- Run the given FuncOfTask in your task.
[CODE]function task:Call(funcOfTask)[/CODE]
-- Continue with the given FuncOfTask after the previous ones have finished.
[CODE]function task:Then(funcOfTask)[/CODE]
-- Set a final handler. Called even if something went wrong or the task was cancelled.
[CODE]function task:Finally(funcOfTask)[/CODE]
-- Set an error handler. Called if something went wrong in your task.
[CODE]function task:OnError(funcOfTask)[/CODE]

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]API: Loops[/COLOR][/SIZE]
-- Start an interruptible for-loop. A negative step counts backwards, a step of 0 is an error.
[CODE]function task:For(from, to, step)
function task:For(pairs(tbl))
function task:For(ipairs(tbl))[/CODE]
-- Execute the loop with the given step function. The parameters are those you would use in your for body.
[CODE]function task:Do(func)[/CODE]
The signature of func is [FONT="Courier New"]function(index)[/FONT] or [FONT="Courier New"]function(key, value)[/FONT]. Return [FONT="Courier New"]async.BREAK[/FONT] within func to break the loop.

-- Start an interruptible while-loop.
[CODE]function task:While(func):Do(doFunc)[/CODE]
As long as func returns true, doFunc and func will be called again.

-- Wait until func returns true. It is checked once per frame. Keep the check simple, for example a flag set by an event.
[CODE]function task:WaitUntil(func)[/CODE]

-- Sort asynchronously. Works like table.sort(), compare is optional.
[CODE]function task:Sort(array, compare)[/CODE]

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]API: Time[/COLOR][/SIZE]
-- Suspend the task immediately for the given delay in milliseconds, then call the given FuncOfTask. Steps still queued wait. A new Delay replaces a running one. Delays below 10 ms are executed immediately as a normal Call.
[CODE]function task:Delay(delay, funcOfTask)[/CODE]
-- Same, but waits at its position in the chain, after the previous steps.
[CODE]function task:ThenDelay(delay, funcOfTask)[/CODE]
-- Stop a delay created by Delay.
[CODE]function task:StopTimer()[/CODE]

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]API: Default task[/COLOR][/SIZE]
Non-interruptible shortcuts, or nested calls if used inside a running step:
[CODE]async:Call(funcOfTask)
async:For(...)
async:While(func)
async:WaitUntil(func)
async:Sort(array, compare)[/CODE]

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]API: Diagnostics[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[CODE]async:SetDebug(true)      -- log frames exceeding the budget and steps slower than 1 ms (via LibDebugLogger, if installed)
async:GetDebug()
async:GetCpuLoad()         -- load of the time budget in percent
async:SetLogToChat(true)
async:GetLogToChat()[/CODE]

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]Example 1: Repeating step[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[highlight="Lua"]local async = LibAsync

local task = async:Create("example1")

local i = 1
local function Hello() d("Hello") end
local function World() d("World") end

task:Call(function(task)
 d(i)
 task:Call(Hello):Then(World)
 i = i + 1
 return i < 1000 -- true: call again
end):Then(function() d("end") end)
[/highlight]
[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]Example 2: Loops[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[highlight="Lua"]local async = LibAsync

local task = async:Create("example2")

local start = GetGameTimeMilliseconds()

task:For(1, 1000):Do(function(index) d(index) end):Then(
function()
  task:For(pairs({"a", "b"})):Do(function(key, value) d(key, value)
    task:For(ipairs({"c", "d"})):Do(function(key, value) d(key, value) end)
 end)
end):For(1001, 1010):Do(function(index) d(index) end):Then(function(task)
  df("%ims", GetGameTimeMilliseconds() - start)
end)[/highlight]
[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]Example 3: While[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[highlight="Lua"]local async = LibAsync

local i = 0
async:While(function() return i < 100 end):Do(function()
  d(i)
  i = i + 1
end)
[/highlight]
[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]Example 4: WaitUntil[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[highlight="Lua"]local async = LibAsync
local time = GetGameTimeSeconds() + 10
async:WaitUntil(function()
  return GetGameTimeSeconds() >= time
end):Then(function()
  d("end")
end)
[/highlight]
[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]Example 5: Nested calls[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[highlight="Lua"]local async = LibAsync

async:Call(function(task)
  d("a")
  task:Call(function(task)
    d("b")
    task:Call(function(task)
      d("c")
    end)
  end)
end):Then(function(task)
  d("d")
  task:Call(function(task)
    d("e")
    task:Call(function(task)
      d("f")
    end)
  end)
end)
[/highlight]
Output: a b c d e f

[SIZE="3"][COLOR="DarkOrange"]Example 6: Error handling, Finally and Cancel[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[highlight="Lua"]local async = LibAsync

local task = async:Create("example6")
task:OnError(function(task)
  d("failed: " .. tostring(task.Error))
end):Finally(function()
  d("cleanup") -- always runs
end):For(1, 100):Do(function(index)
  if index == 50 then error("boom") end
end)

-- or stop it from outside, e.g. when the user closes your window:
-- task:Cancel()
[/highlight]
[SIZE="3"][COLOR="Orange"]Remarks[/COLOR][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]Yes, using LibAsync increases the total duration of the process for a better frame rate.
[*]Lua code can cause hiccups and (micro-)freezes, but this has nothing to do with lag.
[*]Lua can cause a crash to bug report if too much memory is allocated within one frame (e.g. string operations). => Spread your code over time.
[*]Lua can cause a kick to login if too many server requests are done within a time range (e.g. map pings/buy/sell/ignore). => Spread your code over time.
[/LIST]
